{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "How does Spanner use the TrueTime API to guarantee external consistency for distributed transactions?", "answer": "Spanner uses the TrueTime API to guarantee external consistency by directly exposing clock uncertainty (ε) and using it to assign globally-meaningful commit timestamps that reflect serialization order. The TrueTime API returns an interval [t, t+ε] representing the current time with uncertainty ε, which is kept small (typically <10ms) using GPS and atomic clocks.\n\nTo ensure external consistency (linearizability), Spanner enforces a monotonicity invariant: within each Paxos group, timestamps are assigned in monotonically increasing order across leaders, enforced by disjoint timestamp intervals tied to leader leases. Additionally, Spanner enforces an external-consistency invariant: if transaction T2 starts after the commit of T1, then T2’s commit timestamp must be greater than T1’s. If clock uncertainty is large, Spanner slows down to wait out that uncertainty before assigning timestamps.\n\nThis allows Spanner to assign globally-meaningful commit timestamps even for distributed transactions, enabling externally consistent reads and writes, consistent backups, atomic schema updates, and lock-free read-only transactions at global scale.\n\nSources: spanner.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Spanner TrueTime API external consistency distributed transactions", "call_id": "3DffdTavrOyXRTyZVKVvDUZc4x3xUxzx"}, {"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Spanner TrueTime guarantees external consistency transaction ordering", "call_id": "80S5nGyoJqxExf9LrdghsSBoyYSWFoFq"}, {"turn": 1, "type": "final_answer", "content": "Spanner uses the TrueTime API to guarantee external consistency by directly exposing clock uncertainty (ε) and using it to assign globally-meaningful commit timestamps that reflect serialization order. The TrueTime API returns an interval [t, t+ε] representing the current time with uncertainty ε, which is kept small (typically <10ms) using GPS and atomic clocks.\n\nTo ensure external consistency (linearizability), Spanner enforces a monotonicity invariant: within each Paxos group, timestamps are assigned in monotonically increasing order across leaders, enforced by disjoint timestamp intervals tied to leader leases. Additionally, Spanner enforces an external-consistency invariant: if transaction T2 starts after the commit of T1, then T2’s commit timestamp must be greater than T1’s. If clock uncertainty is large, Spanner slows down to wait out that uncertainty before assigning timestamps.\n\nThis allows Spanner to assign globally-meaningful commit timestamps even for distributed transactions, enabling externally consistent reads and writes, consistent backups, atomic schema updates, and lock-free read-only transactions at global scale.\n\nSources: spanner.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 3910, "completion_tokens": 330}
