{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "What is FoundationDB's unbundled architecture and what consistency guarantees does it provide?", "answer": "FoundationDB employs an unbundled architecture that decouples three core subsystems: an in-memory transaction management system, a distributed storage system, and a built-in distributed configuration system. Each sub-system can be independently provisioned and configured to achieve desired scalability, high-availability, and fault tolerance properties. This design separates transaction processing from logging and storage, enabling the horizontal scaling of both read and write handling.\n\nRegarding consistency guarantees, FoundationDB provides strict serializable transactions by default. It combines Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC) and Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) to ensure these strong semantics while scaling to handle large workloads. Unlike traditional NoSQL systems that sacrificed transactional semantics for scalability—often providing eventual consistency—FoundationDB avoids this trade-off. The system allows relaxing strict serializable semantics for applications that do not require them through flexible, fine-grained controls over conflicts. This architecture also enables the implementation of advanced features such as consistent secondary indices and referential integrity checks.\n\nSources: fdb-paper.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1349, "completion_tokens": 216}
