{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "What is FoundationDB's unbundled architecture and what consistency guarantees does it provide?", "answer": "FoundationDB's unbundled architecture decouples three core subsystems: an in-memory transaction management system, a distributed storage system, and a built-in distributed configuration system. Each subsystem can be independently provisioned and configured for scalability, high-availability, and fault tolerance. This modular design allows different layers (e.g., relational, document, or graph databases) to be built on top of the same underlying storage engine.\n\nRegarding consistency guarantees, FoundationDB defaults to strictly serializable transactions across its entire keyspace, providing ACID semantics. It combines Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC) and Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) to ensure strict serializability. While it defaults to strong consistency, the system allows relaxing these semantics for applications that do not require them through flexible, fine-grained controls over conflicts. This combination of strong default guarantees with optional relaxation enables both robust data integrity and high performance.\n\nSources: fdb-paper.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "FoundationDB unbundled architecture consistency guarantees", "call_id": "fVTWWw6lUssS4htdIqoWLYfVzY2wAi9d"}, {"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "FoundationDB ACID transactions strong consistency distributed database", "call_id": "57iJ6Vw1SQFQccJgytdF0j1seSbODlih"}, {"turn": 1, "type": "final_answer", "content": "FoundationDB's unbundled architecture decouples three core subsystems: an in-memory transaction management system, a distributed storage system, and a built-in distributed configuration system. Each subsystem can be independently provisioned and configured for scalability, high-availability, and fault tolerance. This modular design allows different layers (e.g., relational, document, or graph databases) to be built on top of the same underlying storage engine.\n\nRegarding consistency guarantees, FoundationDB defaults to strictly serializable transactions across its entire keyspace, providing ACID semantics. It combines Optimistic Concurrency Control (OCC) and Multi-Version Concurrency Control (MVCC) to ensure strict serializability. While it defaults to strong consistency, the system allows relaxing these semantics for applications that do not require them through flexible, fine-grained controls over conflicts. This combination of strong default guarantees with optional relaxation enables both robust data integrity and high performance.\n\nSources: fdb-paper.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 3519, "completion_tokens": 293}
