{"ts": 12.30081582069397, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1421, "completion_tokens": 251, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "What is TAO and how does it serve Facebook's social graph?", "answer": "TAO (The Facebook Social Graph) is a geographically distributed data store designed specifically for serving Facebook's social graph workload. It replaces memcache for many data types by implementing a read-optimized graph data store that mediates access to persistent MySQL storage with its own graph-aware cache.\n\nTAO serves the social graph by providing efficient and timely access through a simple data model and API tailored for this purpose. The system models entities (people, actions, relationships) as nodes and edges in a graph, allowing flexible representation of real-life objects and application-specific data. It is deployed as a single geographically distributed instance running on thousands of machines, handling many petabytes of data.\n\nKey capabilities include processing a billion reads and millions of writes each second with high throughput and low latency for large, changing social graphs. TAO avoids the shortcomings of lookaside cache architectures by implementing graph abstraction directly. It allows independent design, scaling, and operation of cache and persistent store layers while maximizing component reuse across Facebook's organization. The system maintains availability with a failure rate of approximately 4.9 × 10⁻⁶ over 90 days.\n\nSources: atc13-bronson.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1421, "completion_tokens": 251}
