{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "What is TAO and how does it serve Facebook's social graph?", "answer": "TAO (The All-Things-Ops) is a geographically distributed data store built by Facebook specifically to serve its social graph. It was designed to handle the demanding workload of providing efficient and timely access to the social graph, which includes user relationships, actions, and interests.\n\nBefore TAO, Facebook used MySQL directly with memcache as a lookaside cache. TAO replaced this architecture by implementing a graph abstraction directly, using MySQL only for persistent storage while managing its own graph-aware cache. It is deployed as a single geographically distributed instance across thousands of machines, handling many petabytes of data and processing up to a billion reads and millions of writes per second.\n\nTAO uses a simple data model and API tailored for the social graph, focusing on availability and per-machine efficiency over strong consistency. Its architecture separates caching layers from persistent storage, allowing independent scaling and operation. It supports a fixed set of queries optimized for high-throughput read access to a changing graph.\n\nSources: atc13-bronson.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "TAO Facebook social graph system", "call_id": "Q8W5iCyWJaaEoLwi7tBfopyuRuzohG1E"}, {"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "TAO distributed storage Facebook infrastructure", "call_id": "UOLmUf789npX1iXjdK1STfpy8KZwKO5F"}, {"turn": 1, "type": "final_answer", "content": "TAO (The All-Things-Ops) is a geographically distributed data store built by Facebook specifically to serve its social graph. It was designed to handle the demanding workload of providing efficient and timely access to the social graph, which includes user relationships, actions, and interests.\n\nBefore TAO, Facebook used MySQL directly with memcache as a lookaside cache. TAO replaced this architecture by implementing a graph abstraction directly, using MySQL only for persistent storage while managing its own graph-aware cache. It is deployed as a single geographically distributed instance across thousands of machines, handling many petabytes of data and processing up to a billion reads and millions of writes per second.\n\nTAO uses a simple data model and API tailored for the social graph, focusing on availability and per-machine efficiency over strong consistency. Its architecture separates caching layers from persistent storage, allowing independent scaling and operation. It supports a fixed set of queries optimized for high-throughput read access to a changing graph.\n\nSources: atc13-bronson.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 3867, "completion_tokens": 304}
