{"ts": 12.938155174255371, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1327, "completion_tokens": 282, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "How does Dapper instrument and trace distributed systems at Google?", "answer": "Dapper instruments and traces distributed systems at Google through a design focused on low overhead, application-level transparency, and ubiquitous deployment. The core instrumentation consists of less than 1000 lines of C++ code and under 800 lines in Java, with an additional 500 lines for key-value annotations. This minimal implementation is linked into common libraries rather than requiring extensive application-level modifications.\n\nDapper's daemon is part of Google's basic machine image, ensuring it runs on virtually every server. By instrumenting a small number of common libraries, Dapper achieves near-universal coverage across production processes without noticeable performance impact. The system traces work done on behalf of an original request by tying together all operations at node boundaries and across different software layers.\n\nDapper supports optional application annotations to enrich traces with debugging information like input/output sizes and status messages. While the core instrumentation is minimal, Dapper has evolved into a monitoring platform enabling various analysis tools. The system uses sampling techniques to manage scale effectively. However, there are limitations: Dapper may struggle with non-standard control-flow primitives or attribute causality incorrectly in some cases. For teams needing interactive search over annotations, custom MapReduce jobs or manual inspection is required. Integration with general-purpose debug logs remains challenging due to the volume of data involved.\n\nSources: 36356.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1327, "completion_tokens": 282}
