{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "How does Dapper instrument and trace distributed systems at Google?", "answer": "Dapper instruments distributed systems at Google by using low-overhead instrumentation restricted to a small number of common libraries (less than 1000 lines of C++ and under 800 lines in Java), rather than modifying every application or middleware layer. This approach ensures application-level transparency and allows tracing without noticeable performance impact. The system uses sampling at the trace level—deciding to sample or discard entire traces based on a trace ID—to manage data volume, with a secondary sampling coefficient controlling write rates to repositories. Dapper traces are collected at node boundaries whenever control is passed between different software layers within a node, tying together all work done on behalf of a given original request. The core instrumentation and trace collection daemon are part of Google’s basic machine image, making them present on virtually every server, enabling ubiquitous deployment across nearly every production process without requiring application-level modifications.\n\nSources: 36356.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Dapper instrument trace distributed systems Google", "call_id": "J5vieLv48qvowCD6dh8zGTQJlUdxp2sA"}, {"turn": 2, "type": "search", "query": "Dapper sampling instrumentation libraries Google distributed tracing", "call_id": "IDUbwNQAIaoMbdRyClnYXr2TJl1lgV3l"}, {"turn": 3, "type": "final_answer", "content": "Dapper instruments distributed systems at Google by using low-overhead instrumentation restricted to a small number of common libraries (less than 1000 lines of C++ and under 800 lines in Java), rather than modifying every application or middleware layer. This approach ensures application-level transparency and allows tracing without noticeable performance impact. The system uses sampling at the trace level—deciding to sample or discard entire traces based on a trace ID—to manage data volume, with a secondary sampling coefficient controlling write rates to repositories. Dapper traces are collected at node boundaries whenever control is passed between different software layers within a node, tying together all work done on behalf of a given original request. The core instrumentation and trace collection daemon are part of Google’s basic machine image, making them present on virtually every server, enabling ubiquitous deployment across nearly every production process without requiring application-level modifications.\n\nSources: 36356.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 7614, "completion_tokens": 440}
