{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "What microservice architectural patterns are identified in the systematic mapping study by Taibi et al.?", "answer": "Based on the provided context, the systematic mapping study by Taibi et al. identifies three commonly used architectural patterns for microservices:\n\n1.  **Orchestration and Coordination-oriented architecture patterns:** These capture communication and coordination from a logical perspective.\n2.  **Deployment strategies:** These reflect physical deployment of microservices on hosts through containers or virtual machines.\n3.  **Data-storage patterns:** Specifically, the study highlights \"Database per Service\" as an example of a pattern deemed proper for microservices (cited in related work by Zdun et al.).\n\nThe study concludes that three orchestration and data-storage patterns emerged as widely adopted when implementing microservice-based systems. These patterns were extracted from case studies and literature reports on the usage of microservices. The authors also note that while some patterns are used for migrating existing monolithic applications, there is a lack of thorough investigation into performance, development effort, maintenance, and anti-patterns.\n\nSources: 2018-Closer Architectural patterns for microservices.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1391, "completion_tokens": 219}
