{"ts": 11.332777976989746, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1433, "completion_tokens": 228, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "What does Pivot Tracing provide for monitoring and debugging distributed systems?", "answer": "Pivot Tracing provides a monitoring framework for distributed systems that addresses limitations of traditional tools like logs, counters, and metrics. It allows users to define arbitrary metrics at one point in the system while selecting, filtering, and grouping events meaningful at other parts of the system, even when crossing component or machine boundaries.\n\nKey capabilities include:\n- Dynamic instrumentation with runtime installation\n- A novel \"happened-before join\" operator that contextualizes queries using Lamport's happened-before relation\n- Ability to group and filter events based on properties of causally preceding events\n- Support for both one-off interactive debugging queries and standing long-running monitoring queries\n- Cross-tier analysis between inter-operating applications\n\nThe system is dynamic, extensible, and enables cross-tier analysis with low execution overhead. It can effectively identify diverse root causes such as software bugs, misconfiguration, and limping hardware. Pivot Tracing serves as a foundation for developing further diagnosis tools and integrates monitoring of heterogeneous distributed systems in a uniform and ubiquitous way.\n\nSources: 2015SOSP_PivotTracing.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1433, "completion_tokens": 228}
