{"ts": 12.077875852584839, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1440, "completion_tokens": 260, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "How does Canopy support end-to-end performance tracing at Facebook?", "answer": "Canopy supports end-to-end performance tracing at Facebook by recording causally related performance data across the entire execution path of requests. This includes data from browsers, mobile applications, and backend services. It constructs traces by propagating identifiers through the system to correlate information across different components.\n\nThe system addresses three main challenges:\n1.  **Heterogeneous Data:** It handles various execution models and granularities across Facebook's stack, avoiding the need for engineers to manually map low-level event data to higher-level semantic structures.\n2.  **Real-Time Processing:** Canopy processes traces in near real-time, allowing engineers to query and analyze performance data immediately as issues arise.\n3.  **Customization:** It enables deep customization by users, from sampling traces to extracting and visualizing specific features tailored to the problem they are solving.\n\nCanopy aggregates data across billions of requests daily, deriving user-specified features and outputting them to long-lived datasets. For example, it calculates metrics like page load latency by recording execution in both client browsers and Facebook backends, enabling a global view for diagnosing performance regressions that manifest differently across the stack.\n\nSources: Canopy_An_End-to-End_Performance_Tracing_And_Analy.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1440, "completion_tokens": 260}
