{"ts": 10.089972972869873, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1411, "completion_tokens": 187, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "How does Seer localize the root cause of performance degradations in cloud microservices?", "answer": "Seer localizes the root cause of performance degradations in cloud microservices by combining lightweight distributed RPC-level tracing with detailed low-level hardware monitoring. It leverages deep learning techniques on massive amounts of tracing data to learn spatial and temporal patterns that translate to Quality of Service (QoS) violations. When an imminent QoS violation is detected, Seer diagnoses the source of unpredictable performance. If performance counters are disabled on a public cloud, Seer uses tunable microbenchmarks to determine the source of unpredictable performance. Additionally, it uses specialized tracing levels instead of collecting detailed low-level traces for all active microservices to ensure distributed tracing remains lightweight, reserving detailed hardware tracing only for microservices likely to cause disruptions. Once an issue is identified, Seer provides recommendations to the cluster manager on how to avoid the degradation altogether.\n\nSources: 2019.asplos.seer.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1411, "completion_tokens": 187}
