{"ts": 11.931329011917114, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1376, "completion_tokens": 264, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "What does Sage do for ML-driven performance debugging in microservices?", "answer": "Sage is a practical and scalable ML-driven performance debugging system designed for large-scale cloud applications, particularly microservices architectures. Its primary function is to diagnose the root cause of end-to-end Quality of Service (QoS) violations and apply corrective actions to restore performance. Unlike traditional methods that rely on memorizing per-tier/metric thresholds or require extensive kernel-level instrumentation and data labeling, Sage learns the impact of microservice dependencies.\n\nSage targets performance issues caused by deployment, configuration, and resource provisioning reasons rather than design bugs. It achieves this using lightweight tracing that does not require application changes or kernel instrumentation, making it practical for production environments. The system correctly identifies the specific microservice(s) and system resources that initiated a QoS violation in over 93% of cases.\n\nWhen a performance issue is detected, Sage can take corrective actions such as scaling out problematic microservices on new nodes or migrating them (especially for stateful backends). It significantly outperforms standard autoscalers by understanding the complex dependency graphs between tiers, which helps avoid cascading QoS violations and backpressure. By learning these dependencies instead of relying on static thresholds, Sage improves performance predictability without sacrificing resource efficiency.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sage.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1376, "completion_tokens": 264}
