{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "What does Sage do for ML-driven performance debugging in microservices?", "answer": "Sage performs ML-driven performance debugging in microservices by diagnosing root causes of end-to-end Quality-of-Service (QoS) violations and applying corrective actions. It uses two main techniques: a Causal Bayesian Network (CBN) to capture dependencies between microservices and latency propagation, and a Graphical Variational Autoencoder (GVAE) to generate counterfactual scenarios that test whether tweaking a microservice’s performance restores QoS. Sage identifies the specific microservices and system resources initiating violations without requiring data labeling or kernel instrumentation. Once a root cause is found, its actuator adjusts resources by tuning CPU frequency, scaling up/out, limiting co-scheduled tasks, partitioning cache or network bandwidth, or migrating services if needed. It focuses on deployment, configuration, and resource provisioning issues rather than design bugs.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sage.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Sage ML-driven performance debugging microservices", "call_id": "Xx0sW1ZTqIz6TxH3LhLWFEJwIHffB8X2"}, {"turn": 2, "type": "search", "query": "Sage ML pipeline root cause analysis corrective action microservices dependencies", "call_id": "aC5OeJxr6JoCAZqsaKfUR1qt2rW7P4KQ"}, {"turn": 3, "type": "final_answer", "content": "Sage performs ML-driven performance debugging in microservices by diagnosing root causes of end-to-end Quality-of-Service (QoS) violations and applying corrective actions. It uses two main techniques: a Causal Bayesian Network (CBN) to capture dependencies between microservices and latency propagation, and a Graphical Variational Autoencoder (GVAE) to generate counterfactual scenarios that test whether tweaking a microservice’s performance restores QoS. Sage identifies the specific microservices and system resources initiating violations without requiring data labeling or kernel instrumentation. Once a root cause is found, its actuator adjusts resources by tuning CPU frequency, scaling up/out, limiting co-scheduled tasks, partitioning cache or network bandwidth, or migrating services if needed. It focuses on deployment, configuration, and resource provisioning issues rather than design bugs.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sage.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 7792, "completion_tokens": 432}
