{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "How does Sinan perform QoS-aware resource management for cloud microservices?", "answer": "Sinan performs QoS-aware resource management for cloud microservices by acting as a centralized, data-driven cluster manager that operates online. It leverages scalable and validated machine learning models to infer the performance impact of dependencies between microservices, specifically accounting for backpressure effects and cascading QoS violations. The system dynamically adjusts per-tier resource allocations according to the service's runtime status and end-to-end QoS targets to preserve tail latency requirements.\n\nSinan is implemented with a centralized manager that maintains global visibility into cluster and application state, alongside per-node resource agents that track per-tier performance and utilization. By using these ML models, Sinan allocates resources in a way that meets QoS without sacrificing resource efficiency, unlike prior work which often leads to unpredictable performance or low utilization. The approach is also explainable, allowing cloud operators to gain insights into how to better deploy and design applications.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sinan.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1307, "completion_tokens": 199}
