{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "How does Sinan perform QoS-aware resource management for cloud microservices?", "answer": "Sinan performs QoS-aware resource management for cloud microservices by using a data-driven, online approach that leverages validated machine learning (ML) models to automatically infer how resource allocations impact end-to-end tail latency across microservice dependencies. Unlike previous ML-based systems designed for monolithic services, Sinan accounts for the cascading effects and backpressure introduced by inter-service dependencies in microservice graphs. It operates online, adjusting its decisions dynamically to application changes, ensuring that QoS requirements are met without sacrificing resource efficiency. The system automatically determines the impact of per-tier resource allocations, relieving users or operators from manually inferring complex dependency impacts.\n\nSinan uses two ML models: a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for short-term performance prediction and a Boosted Trees model for long-term performance evolution. This combination allows Sinan to examine near-future outcomes while accounting for system inertia in building queues. It employs an efficient space exploration algorithm to focus on corner cases that may introduce QoS violations, training these models on collected data to predict end-to-end latency and QoS violation probabilities. Based on model outputs, Sinan selects resource allocations that meet QoS targets using the least necessary resources, communicating decisions to per-node agents for enforcement.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sinan.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Sinan QoS-aware resource management cloud microservices", "call_id": "bYbPm3nKeLz2GEnBA5wlCOcyJCkWvwhg"}, {"turn": 2, "type": "search", "query": "Sinan ML models resource allocation microservices dependencies backpressure", "call_id": "NNbb4cW0jHRyJaO2Gr5xpNuupsK8MEyH"}, {"turn": 3, "type": "final_answer", "content": "Sinan performs QoS-aware resource management for cloud microservices by using a data-driven, online approach that leverages validated machine learning (ML) models to automatically infer how resource allocations impact end-to-end tail latency across microservice dependencies. Unlike previous ML-based systems designed for monolithic services, Sinan accounts for the cascading effects and backpressure introduced by inter-service dependencies in microservice graphs. It operates online, adjusting its decisions dynamically to application changes, ensuring that QoS requirements are met without sacrificing resource efficiency. The system automatically determines the impact of per-tier resource allocations, relieving users or operators from manually inferring complex dependency impacts.\n\nSinan uses two ML models: a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) for short-term performance prediction and a Boosted Trees model for long-term performance evolution. This combination allows Sinan to examine near-future outcomes while accounting for system inertia in building queues. It employs an efficient space exploration algorithm to focus on corner cases that may introduce QoS violations, training these models on collected data to predict end-to-end latency and QoS violation probabilities. Based on model outputs, Sinan selects resource allocations that meet QoS targets using the least necessary resources, communicating decisions to per-node agents for enforcement.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sinan.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 7683, "completion_tokens": 526}
