{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "How does Microscope pinpoint performance issues in microservice environments using causal graphs?", "answer": "Microscope pinpoints performance issues in microservice environments by automatically building a causality graph without requiring domain knowledge or application instrumentation. The system collects two primary data types: network connection information between service instances and Service Level Objective (SLO) metrics for each instance. It continuously monitors SLO metrics of the front end within a sliding time window to diagnose anomalies.\n\nTo construct the causality graph, Microscope utilizes real dependencies captured through network connection information rather than relying solely on statistical methods like Granger Causality or lag correlation used by other tools. This approach allows for precise pinpointing of root causes at the service instance level. The system employs a parallelized method to build this graph using both service dependency and service impact graphs co-located on a single machine.\n\nBy inferring root causes along the directed edges in the causality graph, Microscope avoids brute-force searches, significantly reducing the volume of performance metrics that need processing. It operates in real-time mode to adapt to the dynamic nature of microservice environments. Experimental evaluations show it achieves high precision and recall for performance diagnosis while remaining lightweight enough to scale in large distributed systems.\n\nSources: Microscope Pinpoint Performance Issues with Causal Graphs in Micro-s - Lin - 2018 - International Conference on Service-Oriented .pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1528, "completion_tokens": 271}
