{"event": "turn", "turn": 1, "content": "Microscope pinpoints performance issues in microservice environments by automatically building a **causality graph** without requiring domain knowledge or application instrumentation. The system colle", "tool_calls": []}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "How does Microscope pinpoint performance issues in microservice environments using causal graphs?", "answer": "Microscope pinpoints performance issues in microservice environments by automatically building a **causality graph** without requiring domain knowledge or application instrumentation. The system collects two key data types: **network connection information** between service instances and **SLO (Service Level Objective) metrics** for each instance. When an SLO violation is detected at the front end, Microscope triggers a root cause analysis by building a causality graph using network connections to represent real dependencies. It then employs a **conditioned graph traversing algorithm** that starts from the front end and follows directed edges through the entire graph to identify potential root causes. Finally, it calculates a score for each candidate and ranks them to provide a prioritized list of possible root causes. This approach allows Microscope to pinpoint issues at the service instance level in real-time with high precision (average 88% in evaluations) while significantly reducing the volume of metrics processed compared to brute-force search methods.\n\nSources: Microscope Pinpoint Performance Issues with Causal Graphs in Micro-s - Lin - 2018 - International Conference on Service-Oriented.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Microscope microservice performance issues causal graphs", "call_id": "QRR1oyqG7z076E3mHtx9yj2UbwQLW2oV"}, {"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Microscope system causal inference performance debugging", "call_id": "zeDTGewtwM5ZatmB4IaayrNNw3ARR53V"}, {"turn": 1, "type": "final_answer", "content": "Microscope pinpoints performance issues in microservice environments by automatically building a **causality graph** without requiring domain knowledge or application instrumentation. The system collects two key data types: **network connection information** between service instances and **SLO (Service Level Objective) metrics** for each instance. When an SLO violation is detected at the front end, Microscope triggers a root cause analysis by building a causality graph using network connections to represent real dependencies. It then employs a **conditioned graph traversing algorithm** that starts from the front end and follows directed edges through the entire graph to identify potential root causes. Finally, it calculates a score for each candidate and ranks them to provide a prioritized list of possible root causes. This approach allows Microscope to pinpoint issues at the service instance level in real-time with high precision (average 88% in evaluations) while significantly reducing the volume of metrics processed compared to brute-force search methods.\n\nSources: Microscope Pinpoint Performance Issues with Causal Graphs in Micro-s - Lin - 2018 - International Conference on Service-Oriented.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 3588, "completion_tokens": 324}
