{"ts": 13.881136178970337, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1527, "completion_tokens": 333, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "Facebook uses both TAO and a memcache-based caching layer. What are the different roles of TAO and memcache in serving the social graph?", "answer": "TAO and memcache serve distinct roles in Facebook's social graph architecture based on their design goals and capabilities.\n\nMemcache functions as a general-purpose, lookaside cache for key-value data. It is used to store frequently accessed items to reduce database load and latency. However, it operates with a simple key-value model that does not inherently understand the structure of the social graph (nodes and edges).\n\nTAO (The Application Object) is a specialized, read-optimized graph data store designed specifically for the social graph. Its roles differ from memcache in two fundamental ways:\n1.  **Graph Data Model:** TAO implements a native graph abstraction where nodes are identified by fixed-length persistent identifiers (64-bit integers), allowing it to handle complex relationships directly rather than treating them as opaque key-value pairs.\n2.  **Persistence and Mediation:** TAO takes responsibility for mapping its graph model to persistent storage (MySQL) and mediates access to the database. It includes its own graph-aware cache layer, separating the caching logic from the persistence layer.\n\nWhile memcache was previously used aggressively as a lookaside cache before direct MySQL access, TAO replaces memcache for many data types that fit its specific graph model. TAO is deployed as a single geographically distributed instance capable of processing billions of reads and millions of writes per second, handling high-volume updates and simple queries efficiently. Memcache remains part of the infrastructure but TAO provides a more expressive API tailored for serving the social graph directly.\n\nSources: memcache_nsdi13.pdf, atc13-bronson.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1527, "completion_tokens": 333}
