{"event": "turn", "turn": 3, "content": "TAO and memcache serve distinct roles in Facebook's social graph architecture:\n\n**Memcache (Lookaside Cache):**\n- Originally used as a general-purpose key-value cache for storing query results from My", "tool_calls": []}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "Facebook uses both TAO and a memcache-based caching layer. What are the different roles of TAO and memcache in serving the social graph?", "answer": "TAO and memcache serve distinct roles in Facebook's social graph architecture:\n\n**Memcache (Lookaside Cache):**\n- Originally used as a general-purpose key-value cache for storing query results from MySQL\n- Required fetching entire edge lists even when only one edge changed, making it inefficient for list data\n- Was a \"lookaside\" cache where all data mapping and cache-invalidation logic was in client code\n\n**TAO (Graph Data Store):**\n- A dedicated graph-aware data store that directly implements the objects and associations model\n- Replaced memcache for many data types that fit its graph model\n- Provides efficient access to edge lists without requiring full list reloads on single-edge changes\n- Mediates access to MySQL for persistent storage while using its own graph-aware cache\n- Designed specifically for read-heavy social graph workloads, handling billions of reads and millions of writes per second\n\nIn summary, memcache served as a general-purpose lookaside cache for query results, while TAO is a specialized graph data store that directly implements the social graph model to overcome the limitations of key-value caching for graph data.\n\nSources: atc13-bronson.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Facebook TAO memcache social graph caching roles", "call_id": "zUpyzAG5oCT3tRG2iDuH5Wcpze9oQiZz"}, {"turn": 2, "type": "search", "query": "TAO memcache edge lists graph data store Facebook social graph architecture comparison", "call_id": "KPWCkU7Vtn4Q6TKJ50SVp0R4kor7mBES"}, {"turn": 3, "type": "final_answer", "content": "TAO and memcache serve distinct roles in Facebook's social graph architecture:\n\n**Memcache (Lookaside Cache):**\n- Originally used as a general-purpose key-value cache for storing query results from MySQL\n- Required fetching entire edge lists even when only one edge changed, making it inefficient for list data\n- Was a \"lookaside\" cache where all data mapping and cache-invalidation logic was in client code\n\n**TAO (Graph Data Store):**\n- A dedicated graph-aware data store that directly implements the objects and associations model\n- Replaced memcache for many data types that fit its graph model\n- Provides efficient access to edge lists without requiring full list reloads on single-edge changes\n- Mediates access to MySQL for persistent storage while using its own graph-aware cache\n- Designed specifically for read-heavy social graph workloads, handling billions of reads and millions of writes per second\n\nIn summary, memcache served as a general-purpose lookaside cache for query results, while TAO is a specialized graph data store that directly implements the social graph model to overcome the limitations of key-value caching for graph data.\n\nSources: atc13-bronson.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 8453, "completion_tokens": 598}
