{"ts": 13.173085927963257, "event": "chat", "prompt_tokens": 1721, "completion_tokens": 278, "finish_reason": "stop"}
{"event": "answer", "mode": "baseline", "question": "The Cornell microservices papers Seer, Sage and Sinan each use machine learning for different purposes. How do their goals and techniques differ?", "answer": "Seer, Sage, and Sinan utilize machine learning for distinct goals and techniques within microservice environments:\n\n**Seer** aims to proactively predict performance issues in the near future to prevent them. It employs a hybrid CNN+LSTM model. Unlike Sage, Seer requires labeling traces with sources of QoS violations during training, which can be challenging in large-scale clouds where injecting contentious benchmarks is difficult.\n\n**Sage** focuses on reactive detection and correction of QoS violations. Its primary advantage is leveraging unsupervised learning, eliminating the need to label training traces. This makes Sage easier to deploy at scale without application- or kernel-level tracing for outstanding requests. While it cannot avoid violations entirely, it detects issues quickly and applies corrective actions before they amplify across tiers.\n\n**Sinan** targets resource management to meet Quality of Service (QoS) targets without sacrificing efficiency. It operates online, adjusting decisions based on application changes. Sinan uses validated ML models, specifically comparing CNNs against MLPs and LSTMs for short-term predictions, finding CNNs offer better accuracy and speed with smaller model sizes. It evaluates impact allocations on end-to-end tail latency using benchmarks like Hotel Reservation and Social Network.\n\nSources: 2021.asplos.sage.pdf, 2021.asplos.sinan.pdf", "prompt_tokens": 1721, "completion_tokens": 278}
