{"event": "answer", "mode": "agentic", "question": "The Cornell microservices papers Seer, Sage and Sinan each use machine learning for different purposes. How do their goals and techniques differ?", "answer": "Seer, Sage, and Sinan are Cornell microservices papers that use machine learning for distinct goals and techniques:\n\n- **Seer** focuses on performance debugging for microservices. It uses deep learning to identify patterns of common performance issues and help locate and resolve them. Its goal is diagnostic assistance in troubleshooting latency problems.\n\n- **Sage** (not fully detailed in the provided context, but referenced alongside Seer and Sinan) likely addresses resource allocation or optimization, though specific details are less clear from the retrieved chunks. It may use ML for predicting resource needs or optimizing configurations.\n\n- **Sinan** is a scalable, QoS-aware resource manager for interactive microservices. Its goal is to infer the impact of resource allocations on end-to-end tail latency in real-time. Sinan operates online and adjusts decisions based on application changes. It uses validated ML models, including CNNs (Convolutional Neural Networks), MLPs (Multilayer Perceptrons), and LSTMs (Long Short-Term Memory networks), to predict latency impacts. Sinan also employs transfer learning techniques for adapting to topology changes or hardware updates.\n\nIn summary:\n- **Seer** uses deep learning for performance debugging and issue localization.\n- **Sage** likely focuses on resource optimization or prediction (details less clear).\n- **Sinan** uses online ML models (CNN, MLP, LSTM) for real-time QoS-aware resource management in microservices.\n\nSources: kannan_eurosys19.pdf, 2021.asplos.sinan.pdf", "n_searches": 2, "trace": [{"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Cornell microservices Seer Sage Sinan machine learning purpose goal technique", "call_id": "b8jukTZKrnR7GFRMDbY90HQml0BHWDvi"}, {"turn": 0, "type": "search", "query": "Seer Sage Sinan Cornell microservices paper comparison ML applications", "call_id": "eeUCDckoPAWcNrILQTbyWDKjmLPaTsTT"}, {"turn": 1, "type": "final_answer", "content": "Seer, Sage, and Sinan are Cornell microservices papers that use machine learning for distinct goals and techniques:\n\n- **Seer** focuses on performance debugging for microservices. It uses deep learning to identify patterns of common performance issues and help locate and resolve them. Its goal is diagnostic assistance in troubleshooting latency problems.\n\n- **Sage** (not fully detailed in the provided context, but referenced alongside Seer and Sinan) likely addresses resource allocation or optimization, though specific details are less clear from the retrieved chunks. It may use ML for predicting resource needs or optimizing configurations.\n\n- **Sinan** is a scalable, QoS-aware resource manager for interactive microservices. Its goal is to infer the impact of resource allocations on end-to-end tail latency in real-time. Sinan operates online and adjusts decisions based on application changes. It uses validated ML models, including CNNs (Convolutional Neural Networks), MLPs (Multilayer Perceptrons), and LSTMs (Long Short-Term Memory networks), to predict latency impacts. Sinan also employs transfer learning techniques for adapting to topology changes or hardware updates.\n\nIn summary:\n- **Seer** uses deep learning for performance debugging and issue localization.\n- **Sage** likely focuses on resource optimization or prediction (details less clear).\n- **Sinan** uses online ML models (CNN, MLP, LSTM) for real-time QoS-aware resource management in microservices.\n\nSources: kannan_eurosys19.pdf, 2021.asplos.sinan.pdf"}], "prompt_tokens": 3923, "completion_tokens": 431}
